Let a JACK client choose its block size, and never report a size of 0.

JUCE 8.0.14's JackAudioIODevice offers exactly one buffer size - whatever
jack_get_buffer_size() says - and ignores the size handed to open(), so the
audio settings show a single entry and the first-run rule that prefers a
5 ms block has nothing to choose from. JACK lets a client ask the server for
a block size (jack_set_buffer_size); PipeWire's JACK honours it (measured on
PipeWire 1.6: deactivate, set 128, activate -> 128 from the next cycle), and
jackd changes its period for every client, which is what a guitar rig wants.
Offer 32..2048 plus the running size, and request the chosen one in open().

The second half is the bug this was found through. PipeWire's JACK reports
a buffer size of 0 from jack_activate() until its first process cycle, and
open() always deactivates and re-activates, so a reopen - a settings change,
or AudioDeviceManager's own restart after the first launch - read the size
inside that window. The player was then prepared with a block size of 0,
and AudioDeviceManager's CallbackMaxSizeEnforcer, with a maximum of 0,
advanced by 0 samples forever: a full core burnt on the audio thread for the
life of the process (journal: 49.8 s CPU over 56.8 s wall), silence, and a
crash the next time the device was closed under it. Remember the last size
the server did report (or the one open() asked for) and answer with that;
never with 0.
diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_JackAudio.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_JackAudio.cpp
